 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Handles non-uniform octree depths efficiently; top-down approach is intuitive; bitwise operations for child selection are fast [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Recursion depth can be large for deep octrees; division by zero requires handling; relies on IEEE floating-point behavior for stability [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When traversing sparse voxel octrees with non-uniform depth on CPU, as it handles null pointers for empty subtrees without requiring fixed-depth assumptions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When ray directions are guaranteed to be positive (simplification possible); when robustness to zero-direction rays is critical without IEEE guarantees [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Complexity:** The worst case for determining the first node requires 4 comparisons. Each subsequent transition requires 2 comparisons. The longest path involves 4 nodes (3 transitions), totaling 10 comparisons per node transition [S1, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Requires less memory than bottom-up methods like Samet-Net because it does not store neighbor voxel pointers [S1, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On a Silicon Graphics Indigo 2 (MIPS R4000), it was 10.4%, 12.7%, and 6.8% faster than binary tree traversal for scenes with 512, 4096, and 32768 spheres respectively [S1, S5, unofficial]. It outperforms bottom-up methods (Samet, SametCorner, SametNet) and is comparable to or faster than Gargantini [S1, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, no neighbor finding, efficient for full or nearly full octrees [S1, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Recursive implementation may cause stack overflow for very deep trees (though iterative stack-based versions exist) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Parametric Octree Traversal was introduced by Revelles in 2000 in the paper "An efficient parametric algorithm for octree traversal" [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is a top-down algorithm designed to overcome limitations of bottom-up DDA algorithms (like Sung's 1991 algorithm) which often require fixed-depth octrees [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is particularly relevant for Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) where empty subtrees are represented by null pointers [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The algorithm was introduced by J. Revelles, C. Ureña, and M. Lastra in 2000 [S1, S2, S5, unofficial]. It is based on the principles of parametric methods for BSP trees and octrees, differing in the selection of the first sub-voxel using simple comparisons on parameter values [S1, unofficial]. It avoids the neighbor finding process of Samet [S1, S5, unofficial] and the sorting of potentially visited sub-voxels of HERO [S1, S5, unofficial].
 
